 {:seon.task/name "docstrings-carry-no-dates-and-no-old-words"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docstrings in `src` carry dated rulings and sessions' provenance, which git keeps (docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept: nothing is a dated log; AGENTS §7: a docstring says what a var is). 24 `src` files hold an ISO date: `seon.cost` (`units`, `tick-allowance`, `ratchet`: measurement dates), `seon.program` (the reach-index docstring near :1085), `seon.agent` (`run-tests-forked`, the test-plan docstring near :456), `seon.reconcile` (`hold-tx`, near :444), `seon.db` (`collect!`: \"owner, 2026-10-22, simplest first\"), `seon.template` (`with-merge`: \"pure-tests 2026-10-23\"), `seon.check` and others. `seon.root/start!`'s docstring calls `:stall-ms` \"a guard\" (AGENTS §9: a guard is the old word, and the stall check is the clock task `seon.root/one-clock`'s). Correct: each docstring states the rule as it holds, the owner's ruling cited as \"DECISIONS, <title>\" where it explains the rule; the measurement kept without its date; `:stall-ms` described as the stall bound it is. Deletes the dated clauses. A law proves it: no docstring of a `src` var holds an ISO date (read from the compiler's facts, `:var/doc`), so a new one is red."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.main-test/no-docstring-carries-a-date}}
